WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.010
Our next speaker is James Muller, who is Professor
of Political Science at the University of

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.880
Alaska in Anchorage, and Academic Chairman
of the Churchill Center.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.220
Dr. Muller served as a White House Fellow
in the US Department of Education in the Reagan

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.950
Administration.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:19.660
His teaching, research, and writing have,
for many years, focused on the statesmanship

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.690
and writings of Winston Churchill.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:26.920
He has published new editions of 2 of Churchill's
interwar books of Essays, Thoughts and Adventures,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.790
and Great Contemporaries.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:33.290
He is currently at work on a new edition of
Churchill's autobiography, My Early Life,

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:38.040
as well as a book on Churchill's writings,
titled The Education of Winston Churchill.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.360
He is also preparing a new edition of Churchill's
book The River War, an historical account

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.000
of the re-conquest of the Sudan, which will
be republished later this year.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.330
This is also the theme of his talk today,
titled Lessons from Churchill's The River

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.550
War.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:01:11.490
Please welcome to the podium Dr. James Muller.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:26.289
James Muller: Ladies and gentlemen, it's a
delight to be here.

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:42.579
Richard mentioned technology, and I'm just
waiting for this.

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:45.380
President Arn and fellow Churchillians.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.659
I'm delighted to be in Denver for this important
conference.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:57.020
I want to thank Larry Arn, whom I first met
more than 40 years ago, and also Hillsdale

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:03.739
College, which I first visited more than 40
years ago, for bringing us together to learn

00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:06.259
from Winston Churchill.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:12.640
I should say that my interest in Churchill
goes back to about that same era, to Winston

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.400
Churchill's 100th birthday, when I was a graduate
student.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.500
Those of us who were living together out on
the edge of town decided to have a birthday

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.740
party for Winston Churchill.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:29.920
We listened to Edward R. Murrow's record with
Churchill's speeches.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.730
We bought some tomato juice and some vodka,
which was all we could afford, and when our

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.569
guests came we said, "Bloody Mary?"

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:41.500
I think I had a Virgin Mary.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:49.819
But after listening to Churchill's speeches,
we decided that, I decided ought to learn

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:52.360
some more about Churchill.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:59.030
I would add one book to those that have been
suggested among the first one might read,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.790
which is Thoughts and Adventures.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.329
That was the first one I read, and I'm still
hooked.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:12.849
But I agree with Richard Langworth that my
early life is a wonderful place to begin.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:17.269
I always say when someone asks me, "What book
should I read about Churchill?"

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.390
I say, "Read books by Churchill first.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:29.359
There are lots of people writing books about
him, but his own books are the best.

00:03:29.359 --> 00:03:40.560
Now, I'm going to speak about a time in Churchill's
early life, which has been referred to a bit,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.030
up till now.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:49.730
But it's way back in 1898, when Churchill
first went to Africa as a young cavalry officer

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.079
and as a war correspondent.

00:03:52.079 --> 00:03:58.529
He took part in Brittain's campaign to rescue
the Sudan from an Islamist regime.

00:03:58.529 --> 00:04:04.700
His 15 newspaper dispatches from that campaign,
which he wrote in purple ink with a fountain

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.569
pen after long marches through the Sahara
Desert in high summer when the temperature

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:18.810
was more than 110 degrees during the day,
on letter paper he had borrowed from the British

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:27.080
Embassy in Cairo, were published eventually
in the Morning Post in London.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:33.090
After serving in Africa, Churchill left the
Army, and he spent a year writing a book on

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.389
the campaign.

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:42.400
Of the 5 books he wrote before entering parliament
in 1901 at the age of 26, this book is the

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.090
most impressive.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.610
Churchill called it The River War.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.750
Three years after it was published, he abridged
it into a single volume for the 2nd edition

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.740
in 1902.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:05:01.870
Seven chapters disappeared completely, and
passages were cut from every chapter, including

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:06.590
his own adventures and his controversial judgments.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.210
The full text of this book has been out of
print since 1902.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:15.340
Twenty-five years ago, I was on my wedding
trip.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:20.870
I was sitting in the British library, and
I made the discovery that the expensive new

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:27.650
edition of all of Churchill's writings that
I had bought had only an abridged version

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.310
of this book, The River War.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:35.560
If I had known Richard Langworth better, he
would have told me that already.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:41.720
But it was a few months to go before I actually
met him in person.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:48.569
I looked up this edition, this unabridged
2-volume edition, of The River War in the

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:54.349
north reading room of the British library,
and discovered that it had many, many wonderful

00:05:54.349 --> 00:06:01.860
things that I had missed in reading the shorter
version of the book.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.310
This new edition of The River War that I decided
somebody ought to prepare, and I ended up

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:14.289
being the one, 25 years ago, is now finally
nearing completion, and is going to be published

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:19.139
in 2 volumes by St. Augustine's Press.

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:25.180
On these slides, and also in the new edition,
the passages that were left out of the 2nd

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:30.591
edition are going to be in red, while the
passages that remained in the 2nd edition

00:06:30.591 --> 00:06:33.159
after the cuts are in black.

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:39.500
That way, you'll have some sense of what you've
been missing if you've never read the full

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.310
2-volume edition of The River War.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:49.600
Churchill, whose regiment was stationed in
India in 1898, got to the Sudan in time to

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:57.509
ride in the cavalry charge in the climactic
battle of Omdurman across the river from Khartoum.

00:06:57.509 --> 00:07:04.210
Now high civilization in Egypt goes back thousands
of years, even before the glory of Greece

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:05.770
and Rome.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:12.210
It was all made possible by the river, without
which Egypt would be only a desert.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:19.250
The river is the Nile, and its annual miracle
of its life-giving flood captivated men even

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.560
before the time of Cleopatra.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:28.180
I've borrowed Paul Klee's painting as the
background for our slides this afternoon.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.800
Churchill was struck by the beauty of the
sunsets on the Nile.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:39.760
He wrote in The River War, "the wonderful
flame pictures baffle memory and are beyond

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.310
recall."

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:49.139
Egypt is a strip of civilization along the
Nile, broadening out into the delta just before

00:07:49.139 --> 00:07:52.639
it flows into the Mediterranean Sea.

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:58.539
To remind you of the geography of Northeastern
Africa, and incidentally after making this

00:07:58.539 --> 00:08:06.000
map I am impressed forever with Martin Gibert's
efforts at map making.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.879
This 1 map I've made took hours and hours,
and I don't think it's quite as good as Martin's.

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:19.889
But I hope it'll be good enough to give you
an idea of the 2 branches of the Nile River,

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:22.199
the White Nile, and the Blue.

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:28.159
The White Nile begins in the great lakes of
Central Africa, losing much of it's water

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:32.980
through evaporation as it flows leisurely
through the jungle.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:38.170
If you've seen The African Queen, you know
what the jungle is like, choked with tangled

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:41.990
vegetation called sudd on it's way north.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:45.080
The Blue Nile, on the other hand, is quite
different.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.040
It rises in the mountains of Ethiopia.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.720
When the shows melt, it comes rushing downstream
through a canyon.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:59.700
The two branches of the Nile meet at Khartoum,
which means "the elephant's trunk."

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:05.680
Four fifths of the water that flows downstream
from Khartoum comes from the Blue Nile, and

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.290
it carries the silt that makes the Nile Delta
so fertile.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.080
That flow is seasonal.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.670
It accounts for the flood that irrigates the
banks of the river hundreds of miles north

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:19.430
in Egypt.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:26.120
Then only 1 other tributary flows into the
river north of Khartoum, the Atbarah River,

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.600
which dries up into disconnected, stagnant
pools in the summertime.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:37.780
Then the Nile flows through the Sahara Desert
for 800 miles without gaining a single drop

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.120
of water.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:44.880
Here's another map of Egypt and the Sudan,
though this one doesn't have the newly formed

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:52.560
nation of South Sudan, which separated from
the southern third of the Sudan in 2009.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.340
The right to divide the water from the Nile,
which is critical to Ethopia, Egypt, and both

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:05.070
Sudans, remains a matter of contention and
high level negotiation.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:11.060
In the 19th century, slavery was the most
important institution in the economy of the

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.140
Sudan.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:18.210
After the fall of Napoleon, Egypt, which was
nominally part of the Ottoman Empire, decided

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:24.650
to become an imperial power in imitation of
European imperialism.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:28.720
Even the Pharoahs had never been able to conquer
the Sudan.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.670
The Sahara Desert was a daunting obstacle.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:40.370
But in the early 1800s, an Egyptian army managed
to go up the Nile, capture Khartoum, and impose

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.750
a harsh rule on the Sudanese.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:47.620
The Egyptians took over the slave trade.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:53.080
Before long, the Sudanese rose in rebellion
against Egyptian misrule.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:58.790
Muhammad Ahmed, the son of a boat builder,
became a prophet, and he rallied his countrymen

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:03.300
to throw off the invaders corrupt version
of Islam.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:09.490
Proclaiming himself the Mahdi, he aspired
to make the whole world, including Cairo,

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:15.000
Constantinople, and even London, submit to
the true faith.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.760
Churchill thinks the Sudanese had good cause
to rebel, and his treatment of the Mahdi in

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:27.800
The River War is remarkably generous, as this
quotation suggests.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:33.731
The first edition of The River War was illustrated
by Angus McNeill, a fellow officer who, like

00:11:33.731 --> 00:11:38.370
Churchill, had gone to Harrow School and to
Sandhurst.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:43.190
His drawings were left out of the 2nd edition
of the book, and have never been reproduced

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:47.670
in any other edition, but they're going to
reappear in this new one.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.730
The Mahdi's soldier's were called the Dervishes.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:58.510
The term Dervish means a religious medicant
who has taken vows of poverty and austerity.

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:05.880
McNeill here depicts a Dervish spearman wearing
his customary garb, a patched cloak, or jibba,

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.410
and carrying primitive weapons.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:14.640
Meanwhile, the Kadive who ruled Egypt for
the Ottoman sultan, was a spendthrift.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.300
He borrowed huge sums from European lenders.

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:24.430
These debts and the heavy taxes that were
required to pay interest in them provoked

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:34.210
a Nationalist revolt in Egypt, which was led
by an Egyptian army officer named Arabi Pasha.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:41.050
You see, the Europeans, especially the British
and the French, were afraid that if the rebels

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:48.420
reneged on this debt and they came to power,
then all the creditors would lose their money.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:52.980
So the British and French governments agreed
to suppress the revolt.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:58.190
But then the French fleet declined to take
part in the campaign and sailed away from

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.370
Alexandria Harbor.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:06.930
The British bombarded the harbor and put an
end to the rebellion, and Britain became responsible

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.700
for ruling Egypt.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:15.510
Sir Evelyn Baring, later Lord Cromer, was
the British advisor to the Kadive, who ruled

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.260
nominally for the Ottoman sultan.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:25.240
What this meant is that the Kadive had to
follow whatever advice Cromer gave him.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.390
Cromer was later a mentor to the young Winston
Churchill.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:34.830
Cromer tried to put the Egyptian economy on
a sound foundation, and to do that he knew

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:41.290
that Egypt would have to abandon the Sudan
despite the growing power of the Mahdi, because

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:46.760
Egypt simply couldn't afford to keep it.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:53.080
Back in London, the liberal administration
of Prime Minister Gladston urged Egypt to

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:55.140
give up the Sudan.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:14:01.350
But evacuating the Europeans in Khartoum became
urgent as the Mahdist army surrounded the

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.320
capital.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:09.420
Charles Gordon, who had made his name in China,
and had battled the slave trade while he was

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:16.490
governor of the Sudan, was sent back to Khartoum
as the British envoy to save the garrisons.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:21.890
But Gladston sent him reluctantly, and he
sent him without any support.

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:27.380
At first, Gordon's reputation awed the Dervish
army.

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.390
They were afraid that a British arm would
follow.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:37.490
But when they saw that he was only 1 man on
a camel, they tightened their grip on Khartoum.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:43.570
For his part, Gordon was stubborn, and he
tried to hold the capital rather than just

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.620
evacuating the Europeans.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:56.280
His situation in Khartoum became dire, but
Gladston was loathe to lift a finger to help.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:02.790
An Austrian army officer, Rudolph Slatin,
commanded an Egyptian army in Darfur.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:08.810
His soldiers became demoralized because the
army was commanded by a Christian.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:12.470
So he told them he was converting to Islam.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:17.920
But his Egyptians were still no match for
the Dervishes, and Slatin was defeated and

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:20.220
captured by the Mahdi.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:27.560
Slatin, then a prisoner, wrote to Gordon,
who desperately needed assistance from somebody

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:33.790
who had the kind of military experience and
ability that Slatin had, offering to escape

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:38.820
from the Mahdi and to join Gordon in defending
Khartoum.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:46.710
But Gordon rejected Slatin's offer with contempt,
because Slatin had forsaken his God.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:53.890
Churchill, in The River War, criticizes Gordon,
who was admired widely in Britain as a Christian

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:57.720
hero, for rejecting Slatin's offer.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:04.490
Gladston was finally forced to send out, not
1, but 2 relief expeditions to save Gordon,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:09.580
1 going through the desert, and the other
1 along the river.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:13.280
But the expeditions arrived too late.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:17.890
Gordon was put to death by the Dervishes,
and he became a martyr.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:21.220
His death, Churchill says, left the British
discouraged.

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:27.860
The Mahdi was triumphant in Kartoum, and he
ruled over all of the Sudan.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:34.310
A few months later, though, the Mahdi died,
and he was succeeded by Abdullah, who was

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:37.510
a soldier rather than a prophet.

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:40.990
Abdullah became the Khalif, or Khalifa.

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:46.790
Churchill doesn't find him as sympathetic
a figure as the Mahdi, but he admits he did

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:53.170
have some civilized qualities, and he's impressed
with his statecraft.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:59.940
Six years later the empire of the Dervishes
reached its greatest extent, ruling much of

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:06.850
present-day Sudan and South Sudan, and slivers
of Ethiopia and Eretria as well.

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:13.309
Abdulla wasn't actually able to conquer Egypt,
but he did defeat the Egyptians in a war with

00:17:13.309 --> 00:17:17.659
King John, and he was master of the Sudan.

00:17:17.659 --> 00:17:24.110
The British disbanded the old Egyptian army
that had fared so ill at the hands of the

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:25.160
Dervishes.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:29.570
Cromer wanted to reconquer the Sudan, but
not right away.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:37.070
A new Egyptian army began to grow up under
British command, and Herbert Kitchener became

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:43.080
Sirdar of the Anglo-Egyptian army.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:50.190
Kitchener's intelligence chief, General Wingate,
and an Austrial priest in Dervish captivity,

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:56.700
Father Ohrwalder, collected information about
the Mahdi's regime.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:02.269
Not only did this intelligence assist the
eventual campaign to reconquer the Sudan.

00:18:02.269 --> 00:18:09.340
But also with Wingate's help, Ohrwalder published
a book about the Dervishes that shocked European

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:10.650
readers.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:16.899
Slatin published another book, Fire and Sword
in the Sudan after Escaping from the Mahdi's

00:18:16.899 --> 00:18:19.809
Camp, which was translated into English.

00:18:19.809 --> 00:18:26.100
These books helped turn public opinion in
Britain in favor of helping Egypt to reconquer

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.129
the Sudan.

00:18:28.129 --> 00:18:36.190
Meanwhile, the Ethiopians dealt a crushing
defeat to a modern Italian army at Adowa,

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:39.330
damaging the reputation of Europeans in Africa.

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:47.210
Also, a French expedition traversing the continent
from west to east threatened to prevent a

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:55.299
British Cape to Cairo railroad by creating
instead an unbroken blue line of French colonies

00:18:55.299 --> 00:18:58.450
across Africa instead.

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:06.530
So early in 1896, Britain resolved to help
Egypt reconquer the Sudan.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:12.779
The Anglo-Egyptian army with British officers
commanding native Egyptian and Sudanese troops

00:19:12.779 --> 00:19:16.340
crossed the border from Egypt into the Sudan.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:22.779
Kitchener began his campaign by reconquering
the northernmost province of the Sudan, Dongola,

00:19:22.779 --> 00:19:24.509
in 1896.

00:19:24.509 --> 00:19:29.669
The progress of the army was eagerly followed
in Britain.

00:19:29.669 --> 00:19:36.250
Kitchener had the latest weapons, iron-clad
gunboats on the Nile with machine guns and

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:37.250
canons.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:40.450
They followed the army south along the river.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:49.799
McNeill's illustrations show the advances
in naval engineering between 1885 and 1898.

00:19:49.799 --> 00:19:56.100
But it was difficult to ascend the Nile, and
the boats had to be towed, or even disassembled

00:19:56.100 --> 00:20:02.059
and portaged around cataracts of the river.

00:20:02.059 --> 00:20:08.720
Here's one of Angus McNeill's drawings of
local tribesmen who were rounded up, one can

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:16.980
only imagine what that was like, to help pull
the boats up over the cataracts.

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:22.320
As you can see in this satellite photo, the
Nile has a big bend in it.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:27.960
Kitchener decided to avoid an advance along
that difficult stretch of the river by building

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:31.190
a railroad across the Sahara Desert.

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:37.880
Construction of this railroad is one of the
most dramatic stories in Churchill's book.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:43.820
Experts in England told Kitchner he couldn't
build a railway across the Sahara Desert in

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:45.409
time of war.

00:20:45.409 --> 00:20:50.309
Kitchner decided he was going to build it
anyway, and his gamble paid off.

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:52.929
Getting to Khartoum was the hard part.

00:20:52.929 --> 00:20:58.970
Once Kitchener's army reached the Dervish
capital, Abdullah's forces were no match for

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:00.650
them.

00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:07.510
When Kitchener needed more engines than England
could provide, he bought them from America.

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:13.039
In the 1st edition of The River War, but not
in the 2nd, Churchill admits that American

00:21:13.039 --> 00:21:20.960
engines with their interchangeable parts stood
up better than British ones.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:27.649
By Spring 1898, the Anglo-Egyptian army had
reclaimed the Sudan along the Nile up to the

00:21:27.649 --> 00:21:31.549
Atbara River, defeating a Dervish army there.

00:21:31.549 --> 00:21:37.820
Colonel Frank Rhodes, he's the brother of
Cecil Rhodes, and he was wounded later in

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:42.720
the campaign, is the man on the left in this
picture with the pith helmet.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:47.400
He was the original editor of The River War.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:51.350
I don't think he spent as much time on it
as I have.

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:56.179
Churchill, a young cavalry officer serving
in India, had been following the campaign

00:21:56.179 --> 00:21:58.571
attentively, and was eager to be part of it.

00:21:58.571 --> 00:22:04.299
But it was a long way from India, and the
climax of the war was fast approaching.

00:22:04.299 --> 00:22:09.510
Churchill enlisted help from his mother to
get to Egypt.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:18.309
Churchill had had his baptism of fire in Cuba
with Spanish forces in 1895 on his 21st birthday.

00:22:18.309 --> 00:22:21.509
This was his first experience at war.

00:22:21.509 --> 00:22:26.879
I think Mr. Krauthamer got it a little out
of kilter last night.

00:22:26.879 --> 00:22:33.570
Then Churchill had commanded troops for the
queen on the Indian frontier in 1897 in the

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:38.870
difficult border country of Pakistan near
the Afghan border, according to the modern

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.830
borders.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:47.220
This campaign in India was the subject of
his first book, which had just been published.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:53.789
By a stroke of luck, the Prime Minister, Lord
Salisbury, a man who had not always been friendly

00:22:53.789 --> 00:22:59.470
to Churchill's father, happened to read Churchill's
book, and he invited him to Downing Street

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.080
for a chat.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:08.000
He offered to help Churchill, and our author
quickly asked to go to the Sudan.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:13.409
But Kitchener turned down the Prime Minister's
request, just as he had earlier turned down

00:23:13.409 --> 00:23:16.919
the entreaties from Churchill's mother.

00:23:16.919 --> 00:23:24.129
Only because the Anglo-Egyptian army was being
augmented, stiffened, by a few regular British

00:23:24.129 --> 00:23:30.990
troops was Churchill able to take part in
the campaign, despite Kitchener's opposition.

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:36.659
He barely got to Africa in time for the climax.

00:23:36.659 --> 00:23:42.330
Because Churchill was late, he had to hurry
to catch up with his regiment, tag end 21st

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:43.330
Lancers.

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:48.780
As he tells us in the 1st edition of The River
War, but not in the 2nd, he got lost in the

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.259
desert and he had to find his own way.

00:23:52.259 --> 00:23:59.720
Fortunately, he didn't run into any Dervish
scouts while he was alone in the Sahara Desert,

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:02.619
he and his little pony.

00:24:02.619 --> 00:24:05.410
But The River War does teach us plenty about
camels.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.059
I wore my camel tie today.

00:24:08.059 --> 00:24:17.380
For instance, it tells us how long a camel
can go without water, 5 days.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:24.619
Then, if the camel drinks as much as possible
at a good water hole, maybe 5 days more, but

00:24:24.619 --> 00:24:31.840
then the camel will die.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:39.090
When Churchill and the army drew near to Khartoum,
he could hear the drums, and he could hear

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:45.639
the ombya, a horn made from an elephant's
trunk that helped keep up the good order and

00:24:45.639 --> 00:24:48.490
the morale of Abdullah's army.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:55.140
He was also impressed by the Dervish's battle
flags, which marked parts of the army commanded

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:58.070
by subordinate khalifas and emirs.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:02.700
They reminded him of the banners he had seen
in the Bayeux Tapestry.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:06.369
The climax of the campaign was the battle
of Omdurman.

00:25:06.369 --> 00:25:12.539
The sirdar's gunboats and machine guns made
it an unequal battle for the Dervishes.

00:25:12.539 --> 00:25:15.679
They were mown down by the hundreds.

00:25:15.679 --> 00:25:21.780
Most of the British casualties, which were
far fewer, took place among the 21st Lancers,

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:26.440
who were ordered to charge into a water course
full of Dervishes.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:30.230
In the hand-to-hand fighting, the odds were
much more even.

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:36.409
Churchill, who fought with a brand new Mauser
pistol instead of a sword because he'd injured

00:25:36.409 --> 00:25:44.020
his shoulder in India, suffered no harm to
himself or to his horse.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:49.429
Even before the Anglo-Egyptian army entered
the Dervish capital of Omdurman across the

00:25:49.429 --> 00:25:55.450
river from Khartoum, Kitchener's gun boats
had done serious damage to the tomb of the

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:56.659
Mahdi.

00:25:56.659 --> 00:26:03.220
Targeting the tomb, which of course was the
most notable and revered building in the capital,

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:07.929
was controversial, and Churchill was one of
the critics.

00:26:07.929 --> 00:26:14.779
In the 1st edition of The River War, Churchill
writes a lengthy critique of Kitchener's bombardment

00:26:14.779 --> 00:26:16.570
of the tomb.

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:24.249
Like many of the criticisms he wrote, it softened
in the abridged 2nd edition.

00:26:24.249 --> 00:26:33.049
Churchill, who had noticed how the British
treated native religions in India with forbearance

00:26:33.049 --> 00:26:43.629
and respect, thought it was a mistake to depart
from that prudent policy in the Sudan.

00:26:43.629 --> 00:26:50.889
Kitchener had the Mahdi's skull made into
an inkwell, which he was happy to use until

00:26:50.889 --> 00:26:58.889
Queen Victoria and Lord Cromer insisted on
having it properly re-buried in Wadi Halfa.

00:26:58.889 --> 00:27:04.460
Churchill argues that if the British are going
to treat the Sudanese with savagery, then

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:08.490
they're no better than the Dervishes.

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:14.460
Eventually, after the 2nd World War, the British
allowed the Sudanese to rebuild the tomb of

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:19.239
the Mahdi, which became a place of pilgrimage.

00:27:19.239 --> 00:27:26.429
Notice the little emblem on the cupola of
the tomb on the right-hand side, because it

00:27:26.429 --> 00:27:31.940
will figure in the story a little later.

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:37.179
Because of his criticisms of Kitchener for
this and many other reasons, including the

00:27:37.179 --> 00:27:44.369
treatment of the wounded on both sides, the
British and the Sudanese, Churchill damns

00:27:44.369 --> 00:27:49.990
Kitchener by faint praise in the 1st edition
of The River War.

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:53.160
You can see this.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:57.780
He says he occupies "certainly the third,
and possibly the second place."

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:04.980
I think he thought Cromer certainly had the
1st place, and perhaps Churchill meant that

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:10.659
maybe Lord Salisbury, who had allowed it all,
and to whom he dedicated the 1st edition of

00:28:10.659 --> 00:28:16.700
The River War, had the 2nd place, leaving
Kitchener a poor third.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:50.190
But in the 2nd edition, as you can read, he
softens that judgment.

00:28:50.190 --> 00:28:55.749
Two days after the battle, a memorial service
was held for Charles Gordon in the ruins of

00:28:55.749 --> 00:29:00.960
his palace in Khartoum, where Gordon's favorite
hymn Abide With Me was sung.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:07.429
At that service, for the 1st time during the
campaign, the British raised a Union flag

00:29:07.429 --> 00:29:10.499
larger than the Egyptian one.

00:29:10.499 --> 00:29:14.999
But Churchill slept through it.

00:29:14.999 --> 00:29:22.369
I thought you might enjoy seeing the title
pages and the new frontispieces of the 2 volumes

00:29:22.369 --> 00:29:26.659
of the new edition of The River War.

00:29:26.659 --> 00:29:32.870
I'm grateful to Lady Soames who wrote the
foreword to the new edition; to Richard Langworth,

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:38.970
who encouraged this project from the very
beginning; to Larry Arne who, before he was

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:44.490
president of Hillsdale, arranged for both
editions of the book to be scanned and offered

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:51.059
funding from the Equis Institute; to Bruce
Fingerhut, my publisher at St. Augustine Press;

00:29:51.059 --> 00:29:57.090
to Allen Packwood and his staff at the Churchill
Archive Center for all their help and research;

00:29:57.090 --> 00:30:02.960
to my collaborators Paul Courtney and Erica
Chenoweth; and to the Churchill Center for

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:06.909
steady support and assistance.

00:30:06.909 --> 00:30:12.960
Three days after the battle, Churchill rode
over the battlefield with a Scottish officer,

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:16.670
Lord Tullibardine, the son of the Duke of
Atholl.

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:23.549
In 1 of the most unforgettable chapters in
the book, which is entirely omitted from the

00:30:23.549 --> 00:30:30.539
2nd edition, Churchill describes the suffering
of the wounded Dervishes, who were neglected,

00:30:30.539 --> 00:30:37.400
and still dying on the battle field 3 days
after the battle in that heat.

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:44.419
His colleague was able to extract the bullet
from the knee of 1 of them as they went on,

00:30:44.419 --> 00:30:51.279
and Churchill and Lord Tullibardine tried
to give water to the sufferers on the battlefield

00:30:51.279 --> 00:30:55.619
until their canteens ran dry.

00:30:55.619 --> 00:31:01.960
Later Tullibardine enlisted help from his
friends, not including Churchill as far as

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:09.320
I can see, in carrying spoils of the campaign
back to his castle in the Scottish Highlands,

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:13.419
where they decorate the banqueting hall.

00:31:13.419 --> 00:31:19.649
He also removed a copper emblem from the top
of that cupola of the Mahdi's tomb, and installed

00:31:19.649 --> 00:31:22.509
it on the clock tower of his castle.

00:31:22.509 --> 00:31:29.100
His letter to the family shows where the emblem
was taken down from the top of the tomb.

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:35.429
Unfortunately, later his clock tower burned
down and the emblem melted in the fire.

00:31:35.429 --> 00:31:41.129
So what you see in the photo is a reconstruction
of the tower and a replica of the emblem that

00:31:41.129 --> 00:31:46.940
used to be at the top of the tomb in Omdurman.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:53.070
Churchill left the Sudan soon after the battle,
taking Nile steamers and the desert railway

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:54.070
north.

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:57.480
At a station in the desert the train broke
down.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:03.789
Wen the Arab engine driver attributed the
delay to Allah, discerning some wise purpose

00:32:03.789 --> 00:32:10.350
in the delay, a lieutenant, or as the British
say, a leftenant of the Grenadier guards who

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:16.879
had experience driving trains, Frederick Harvey
Bathurst, rolled up his sleeves and got the

00:32:16.879 --> 00:32:19.210
engine moving again.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:25.669
Some of the officers thought that conduct
was unbecoming to a British gentleman.

00:32:25.669 --> 00:32:30.679
But Churchill admires his ability.

00:32:30.679 --> 00:32:38.059
This episode provoked a Churchillian effusion
in a chapter of the 1st edition that is omitted

00:32:38.059 --> 00:32:59.080
in the 2nd edition.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:05.039
This quotation has flown around the internet
nonstop ever since 9-11.

00:33:05.039 --> 00:33:06.869
People have looked it up in The River War.

00:33:06.869 --> 00:33:08.649
They can't find it.

00:33:08.649 --> 00:33:11.409
That's because they only have the abridged
version of the book.

00:33:11.409 --> 00:33:16.700
Of course, this passage would now be considered
politically incorrect.

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:21.640
Churchill may have left it out of the 2nd
edition because he thought it impolitic.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:25.760
After all, the British Empire had many Muslim
subjects.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:31.279
Last year a British politician of sorts was
even arrested for quoting this passage in

00:33:31.279 --> 00:33:33.119
public.

00:33:33.119 --> 00:33:35.889
Britain doesn't have a 1st Amendment.

00:33:35.889 --> 00:33:42.059
But look, if you read the quotation, it's
hard to object to Churchill's criticism of

00:33:42.059 --> 00:33:45.239
people who treat women as property.

00:33:45.239 --> 00:33:50.820
The passage, which people looked for in vain
in the abridged edition, will be restored,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:52.869
of course, in the new one.

00:33:52.869 --> 00:33:58.609
The train did get moving again and Churchill
returned to Cairo with a fellow officer who

00:33:58.609 --> 00:34:00.070
had been wounded.

00:34:00.070 --> 00:34:04.739
In My Early Life, you can read the story of
how Churchill had to have a little bit of

00:34:04.739 --> 00:34:22.270
skin from his arm sawed off to help provide
a skin graft to Richard Molyneux.

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:38.480
I wanted you to read this because here's another
passage, this time from an early chapter of

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:44.120
The River War, which did survive the editing,
with Churchill's curious spelling of development,

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:50.120
which was changed in the 2nd edition, and
a drawing by Angus McNiell in the 1st edition

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.040
that some people may think no less objectionable.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:58.800
In fact, some people have argued that this
passage and others like it show that Churchill

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:00.700
was a racist.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:04.550
The truth is more complicated and more o Churchill's
credit.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:12.550
He begins The River War by reflecting the
usual Victorian prejudice against black people.

00:35:12.550 --> 00:35:18.300
But then, as the book goes on, he deliberately
undermines it.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:25.500
By the end of the book, having rejected the
usual prejudice, Churchill argues that the

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:32.550
10th Sudanese, which is a black regiment in
the Anglo-Egyptian army, ranks with the best

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:36.820
troops of the Empire, British or native.

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:41.410
I've highlighted that passage, but let me
show it to you bigger, and I hope some of

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:44.070
you can read it.

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:51.110
You'll see that in this Churchill even argues
that the charge these black troops made in

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:57.830
the 10th Sudanese in a subsequent battle of
the war after Churchill and most of the British

00:35:57.830 --> 00:36:11.250
troops had returned to the UK, was finer than
the charge of the 21st Lancers at Omdurman.

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:18.650
Against the usual view among British officers
that black soldiers wouldn't make good cavalry

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:26.980
troopers, Churchill includes a footnote approving
of the formation of black squadrons of cavalry.

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:33.660
His aim is to weaken prejudice by offering
examples that disprove it, and encouraging

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:41.800
opportunities that have been denied to black
troops on account of prejudices up till now.

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:49.130
To return to the Dervish soldiers, Churchill
denies that they were mad fanatics for pitting

00:36:49.130 --> 00:37:06.660
their trashy weapons against the machine guns
and the gun boats of a far more advanced enemy.

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:17.520
You see what he says?

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:22.850
He claims that these were as brave men as
ever walked the earth.

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:27.790
He claims that they fought to the death because
they were noble and honorable.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:33.080
His magnanimity to a defeated enemy was controversial
when he published it.

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:36.040
But it remained his lifelong view.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:38:06.430
Here is Churchill, much older, inspecting
bomb damage in London during the Blitz.

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:16.540
Here on the left in red is Churchill, with
Churchillian bravery and oratory, in his 20s,

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:23.310
in the pages of The River War, anticipating
Britain's finest hour more than 4 decades

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:28.090
before he became Prime Minister.

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:33.980
Churchill spent a year after the campaign
writing The River War, using his dispatches

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:39.230
from the Morning Post as raw material, but
also studying the history of the Sudan.

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:45.110
He considered how Britain had changed its
future by the reconquest, and he asked whether

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:49.630
that change would help or hurt the people
of the Sudan.

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:57.370
He is lukewarm about imperialism, grand projects
of development, higher education, Christian

00:38:57.370 --> 00:39:02.390
missionary work, and grasping entrepreneurs
in the Sudan.

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:10.100
He thinks what the country needs is very small
development projects like digging wells, and

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:16.490
that the country is going to need a long time
to recover from the war.

00:39:16.490 --> 00:39:20.610
Churchill's adventures in the South African
war where he was captured and imprisoned by

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:28.260
the Boers, but managed to escape and return
to friendly territory, made him famous.

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:32.920
Before long he was elected to the House of
Commons, where he took his seat as a member

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:39.040
of parliament for Oldham early in 1901.

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:45.040
But look at this, more than half a century
later, nearing the end of his political career,

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:52.440
in his second prime ministry, in October 1952,
Churchill welcomed the posthumous son of the

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:59.220
Mahdi, who left 1 of his new wives pregnant
when he died.

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:06.420
This man came to #10 Downing Street in anticipation
of Sudanese independence, which was granted

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:10.640
on January 1st, 1956.

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:18.670
Since then, the Sudan has had a succession
of regimes, 1 of which in the '80s included

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:20.570
other descendants of the Mahdi.

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:26.800
I'm afraid most of those regimes have been
thuggish, terrible regimes, including the

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:30.640
one that's in power right now.

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:32.130
Thank you for your kind attention.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:43.750
I think the floor is now open for questions.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:41:09.400
You know, I think this book is a wonderful
opportunity for somebody who wants to understand

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:16.880
one of the challenges that Charles Krauthamer
was talking about that America faces now.

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:23.540
I know it's required reading in some of our
nation's military colleges.

00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:28.910
I going to the US Naval War College next week
to speak about it.

00:41:28.910 --> 00:41:37.390
Speaker 3: What was the most surprising thing
you've learned after doing this new edition?

00:41:37.390 --> 00:41:39.470
What was 1 thing that stood out?

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:42.450
James Muller: Well, there are many interesting
things I learned.

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:44.930
I learned a lot about how Churchill writes.

00:41:44.930 --> 00:41:53.220
I learned, for instance, that mostly he writes
on the basis of what he remembers.

00:41:53.220 --> 00:42:00.930
Though he did look up some of these quotations
and references, mostly he simply remembered

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:02.820
them.

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:06.870
His memory was amazing, as had been his father's.

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:09.490
His father had almost a photographic memory.

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:11.200
But it wasn't perfect.

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:15.300
So occasionally he gets a quotation a little
bit wrong.

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:20.070
Very often, he doesn't tell you where the
quotation comes from, and it's been my ambition

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:26.370
to try to track down the source of every quotation
in the book, and to give identifications in

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:31.200
footnotes of everyone Churchill mentions in
the book, which has occasioned a great deal

00:42:31.200 --> 00:42:34.690
of detective work, and it's been quite a lot
of fun.

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:40.220
What I've discovered there is that Churchill
doesn't ever want to quote a passage that

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:46.850
isn't written up to his own standard, and
so often he will improve the English of the

00:42:46.850 --> 00:42:50.320
writing when he quotes something.

00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:51.970
So that was one interesting thing I learned.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:54.010
I mean, there were many others.

00:42:54.010 --> 00:43:02.570
I think learning about the circumstances of
this quotation on Islam, which Churchill wrote

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:09.530
when he was sort of frustrated by being stuck
in the desert, was an interesting thing to

00:43:09.530 --> 00:43:10.530
discover.

00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:18.360
Of course, tracking down the dispatches that
Churchill wrote, which were the original version

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:22.020
of some of the parts of the book, was interesting,
too.

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:28.110
Those were not available or known in their
manuscript version when the official biography

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:29.220
was written.

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:38.000
They appeared, finally, in the 1980s, in the
late 1980s, in the archive at the University

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:40.400
of Leeds.

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:43.910
Of course, the dispatches had been published
in the newspapers, and they'd been published

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:47.450
again several times in book form.

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:52.620
But the book version was based only on the
newspaper version, and the newspaper editor

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:57.940
changed what Churchill rote in many, many
ways and left out things.

00:43:57.940 --> 00:44:03.580
So in the new edition there's quite a long
appendix in the 2nd volume that has, for the

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:11.140
1st time, the full version of the dispatches
as Churchill wrote them, published and compared

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:14.520
to the newspaper version.

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:19.140
Those of you who know all about The River
War and have read the 2-volume version will

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:21.520
find that appendix very interesting.

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:27.720
Unless you have been to Leeds and read the
original hand-written dispatches there.

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:29.250
Fourteen of them survive.

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:32.350
One of them doesn't.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:34.950
That was certainly a wonderful discovery.

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:37.310
I discovered it when I was in London.

00:44:37.310 --> 00:44:44.520
I got on a train to Leeds, not knowing how
far it was to Leeds.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:50.550
Had to come back that night to get my suitcases
to spend another couple of days transcribing

00:44:50.550 --> 00:44:52.490
those 15 dispatches.

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:59.950
Then I had a student who learned to read Churchill's
handwriting very well by hard knocks, who

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:08.130
helped me proofread it and make sure we got
every comma and misspelled word right.

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:13.280
Churchill's spelling wasn't too great when
he wrote these dispatches.

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:23.740
Speaker 4: Churchill is mentioned, along with
Lincoln and Washington.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:31.940
In books on Lincoln I have noticed metaphors,
biblical metaphors in his writings, and comments

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:35.500
on Christianity.

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:38.470
We've heard comments on Islam by Churchill.

00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:45.020
What did he have to say about Christianity,
or his understanding?

00:45:45.020 --> 00:45:50.470
James Muller: Churchill tells us in The River
War, which was written much later, in the

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:58.040
1930s, that in his youth he went through a
violent anti-religious phase, which if it

00:45:58.040 --> 00:46:01.770
had continued, would have made him obnoxious.

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:05.390
In The River War you can see signs of that.

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:12.570
He mentions that Christianity, if it had not
been sheltered in the strong arms of science,

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:18.620
against which it struggled in vain, might
have been no match for the military forces

00:46:18.620 --> 00:46:20.870
of Islam.

00:46:20.870 --> 00:46:23.040
He has some other things to say, too.

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:31.070
I mentioned his reservations about Gordon's
confidence that because he was a Christian

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:37.220
hero his martyrdom would somehow be repaid
in heaven.

00:46:37.220 --> 00:46:43.420
Churchill seems to think this was a most imprudent
way of trying to save the lives of the Europeans

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:49.450
in Khartoum, most of whom perished when the
city was taken over by the Dervishes.

00:46:49.450 --> 00:46:58.300
Later, of course, Churchill softened his statements
on Christianity, and that's a long, interesting,

00:46:58.300 --> 00:47:06.280
and complicated subject, about which our next
speaker has spoken after careful research.

00:47:06.280 --> 00:47:08.470
I don't know if he'll talk about it today.

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:16.340
But I recommend his talk, if you get hold
of it, on that subject.

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:21.520
Though I probably have a few disagreements
with bits of it.

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:22.770
Speaker 5: Sir?

00:47:22.770 --> 00:47:24.030
James Muller: Yes.

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:34.060
Speaker 5: I wonder if Winston Churchill ever
forecast or speculated on general warfare

00:47:34.060 --> 00:47:42.370
between Islamic armies and the armies of the
Christian, or Judeo-Christian West.

00:47:42.370 --> 00:47:48.620
James Muller: Well, I think he was lucky enough
to live in a time when the aspirations, let's

00:47:48.620 --> 00:47:57.250
say, of the Mahdi to take over the whole world
and submit it to Islam were obviously unrealistic.

00:47:57.250 --> 00:48:03.090
I think Queen Victoria, when she got his letter
asking her to renounce Christianity and become

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:06.730
a Musim, probably had a good laugh about it.

00:48:06.730 --> 00:48:15.470
But Churchill certainly later in his career,
and not much later, was very interested in

00:48:15.470 --> 00:48:22.900
the reforms that were attempted by those who
called themselves the Young Turks in Turkey,

00:48:22.900 --> 00:48:29.660
to try to establish a Turkish state on a secular
basis, a state that would have a majority

00:48:29.660 --> 00:48:36.790
Muslim population, an overwhelmingly majority
Muslim population, but with strong Christian

00:48:36.790 --> 00:48:47.760
population and populations of other religions,
Zoroastrians and various branches of Islam

00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.080
and Christian believers.

00:48:49.080 --> 00:48:55.350
He was discouraged, of course, when the Turk
chose the wrong side in the 1st World War,

00:48:55.350 --> 00:49:03.040
perhaps somewhat pleased by the eventual launching
of the Turkish Republic under Ataturk, and

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:12.220
I think would be watching closely the changes
that are going forward in Turkey right now.

00:49:12.220 --> 00:49:17.660
Speaker 6: This will be our final question.

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:24.660
Speaker 7: Your exhaustive research, I've
just got 1 quick question for you.

00:49:24.660 --> 00:49:29.810
Given that Winston Churchill, his early career,
he had to have interactions, understandings

00:49:29.810 --> 00:49:34.670
of Cecil Rhodes, what can you tell us about
that interaction?

00:49:34.670 --> 00:49:40.960
What was Churchill's opinion of Rhodes with
the control over Cape Colony, aka South Africa?

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:51.020
James Muller: I went to South Africa in 1993,
just at the time when the changes in the country

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:53.520
were about to occur.

00:49:53.520 --> 00:50:02.280
What had been the house in Cape Town of the
President of South Africa, Declerc, which

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:10.090
was the house Cecil Rhodes built for himself
in Cape Town, with its 14-foot long granite

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:17.640
bathtub on the 2nd floor, 1 can only imagine
how many men had to haul that up the stairs

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:20.210
to install it.

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:26.130
That house was about to become Nelson Mandela's
house.

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:35.020
I was interested in Churchill's contact with
Cecil Rhodes, who was, of course, very important,

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:42.480
not just for Rhodes scholarships in America,
but even more for the British Empire.

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:45.760
Churchill didn't know Cecil Rhodes much.

00:50:45.760 --> 00:50:52.070
He knew his brother because of his contact
with him in this campaign, very much better.

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:59.460
But when he went to South Africa, he had a
number of meals at Rhodes' house.

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:03.780
Cecil Rhodes wasn't there then, though.

00:51:03.780 --> 00:51:11.950
Churchill did have a great deal to do with
the settlement between Boers and Britains

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:20.581
who had launched 2 colonies each on the southern
tip of Africa in previous centuries, after

00:51:20.581 --> 00:51:26.030
the South African war, which he participated
in.

00:51:26.030 --> 00:51:36.660
One of his regrets was that 1 of the differences
between the Boers and the Britains was their

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:40.430
treatment of the indigenous population.

00:51:40.430 --> 00:51:45.901
The compromise that was made with the Boers,
in order to form the Union of South Africa

00:51:45.901 --> 00:51:55.220
in 1906, was to some degree at the expense,
of course, of the black citizens of the country.

00:51:55.220 --> 00:52:05.740
That wasn't really remedied until much, much
later, until the 1990s.

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:12.110
Unfortunately, the results of that in terms
of making a regime that guarantees the rights

00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:18.790
and liberties of the people of that country,
are very mixed.

00:52:18.790 --> 00:52:28.010
More successful, probably, than in the Sudan,
but still not very encouraging.

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:33.160
This idea that people should be ruled by a
tyrant of their own race, color, and creed,

00:52:33.160 --> 00:52:41.500
and that that's adequate to secure happiness
and to protect liberty, is just plain silly.

00:52:41.500 --> 00:52:48.750
We've got to get over that before the countries
of Africa, which deserve the opportunity for

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:57.410
self-government and for freedom, including
freedom of commerce, have a chance to develop

00:52:57.410 --> 00:53:00.910
and improve themselves in the way they can.

00:53:00.910 --> 00:53:06.390
I think 1 of the most notable and obvious
things about Churchill's book is that though

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:14.520
he's not rosy eyed or simple minded about
the obstacles that Africans, and specifically

00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:20.770
people in the Sudan will face in bringing
their country into the modern world, he thinks

00:53:20.770 --> 00:53:26.430
it's possible, and he thinks it's possible
for them to do it.

00:53:26.430 --> 00:53:31.370
He thinks they would have done it even if
the British had not intervened.

00:53:31.370 --> 00:53:35.130
It would have taken longer, he admits.

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:40.320
He's of 2 minds about whether it would have
worked out better.

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:47.690
But at any rate, he thinks Britain has a very
serious obligation now, in 1898, after the

00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:52.990
reconquest, to do its best for the people
of the Sudan.

00:53:52.990 --> 00:53:59.650
He talks about that in the last chapter of
the book, which is left out of the 2nd edition.

00:53:59.650 --> 00:54:00.609
Thank you very much.

